# The three-valued x = y of two intervals of the round-up interval: yes when both are the same
# point, no when they are disjoint, both false when an operand is empty; the default
# interval_equal_1 on the meanings. Exact, in every rounding mode (docs/developer/design/upward.md,
# "Predicates and comparisons"). Requires the rounding mode upward set by kairn::upward::Scope and
# the options of docs/concepts/upward.md (GCC and Clang -frounding-math, MSVC /fp:strict).
kernel:        upward_equal_1
rounding:      upward
input:         pair stored x = xu xn
input:         pair stored y = yu yn
output:        mask yes
output:        mask no
requires:      upward xu xn
requires:      upward yu yn
ensures:       trit yes no eq x y x=xu,xn y=yu,yn exact
min_precision: 1
source:        the lanes compared with the negated lanes (the listing of the round-up interval)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        proved
domain:        extended
ieee_only:     yes
ieee_status:   proved
ieee_theorem:  proofs/theories/IeeeUpwardPredicates.v upward_equal_1_correct
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   the result is a mask, which has no non-finite value, and a comparison with NaN is false; trit states what comes out for every canonical operand

nx = neg(xn)
ny = neg(yn)
ea = eq(nx, xu)
eb = eq(xu, ny)
ec = eq(ny, yu)
eab = and(ea, eb)
yes = and(eab, ec)
da = lt(xu, ny)
db = lt(yu, nx)
no = or(da, db)
